+# Case-Insensitive Matching Folds Case in the Query
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+`REQ-VET-003` narrows the veterinarian directory to one specialty, matched on the whole name ignoring letter case, on every supported database. The three vendor schemas type the specialty name differently. H2 and PostgreSQL compare it case-sensitively; MySQL compares it through a case-insensitive default collation.
+
+The project already reaches case-insensitivity through the schema elsewhere. Pet-name uniqueness uses a different mechanism per vendor ([Database-Enforced Pet Name Uniqueness](2026-07-31-database-enforced-pet-name-uniqueness.md)). Owner search relies on column collation alone, and it is case-sensitive under PostgreSQL — a recorded defect.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Fold case through the schema.** Retype the column per vendor: `VARCHAR_IGNORECASE` under H2, a case-insensitive type or collation under PostgreSQL. Three scripts change, and the guarantee depends on each staying aligned.
+2. **Fold case in the query** (chosen). Apply the same case function to both sides of the comparison in the repository query. No schema changes, and the behavior is identical on every vendor by construction.
+3. **Fold case in application code.** Load every veterinarian and compare in Java. This breaks paging, because the page count would no longer come from the database.
+
+## Decision
+
+A query that matches text ignoring letter case applies one case function to both the column and the parameter. It compares by equality, never by pattern. It does not depend on the column's collation.
+
+The trade-off is index use: a plain index on the name column does not serve the folded comparison. The specialty table holds a handful of rows, so a scan costs nothing measurable.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The specialty filter matches identically on H2, MySQL, and PostgreSQL with no schema change.
+- The H2-backed default suite exercises the same comparison the other vendors run. The vendor integration tests confirm it where Docker is available.
+- Owner search keeps its collation-based matching and its PostgreSQL defect. This decision offers a repair path without taking it.
+- A future case-insensitive match on a large table needs a functional index per vendor to stay fast.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md § Persistence](../system-design.md#persistence) — where query-level case folding is recorded
+- [Script-Managed Schema Across Three Database Vendors](2026-07-31-script-managed-multi-vendor-schema.md) — why the schemas diverge
