+# Narrowed Veterinarian Reads Bypass the Cache
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+The veterinarian repository caches its reads in one cache, declared with no size limit and no eviction. Until `REQ-VET-003`, every cache key came from the page number alone.
+
+The specialty filter adds a free-text value chosen by any caller. Every distinct value, including every letter-case variant of a real name, would become its own cache entry. The application has no authentication, so any client can mint entries without limit.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Cache narrowed reads as they arrive.** Symmetric with the unnarrowed reads, but the cache grows with every distinct value a caller sends.
+2. **Cache narrowed reads under a normalized key.** Folding case shrinks the key space for real names. It does nothing for arbitrary values, which still add one entry each.
+3. **Bound the cache.** A size limit needs configuration specific to the JCache provider, which the project does not carry today. It also lets arbitrary values evict the useful entries.
+4. **Do not cache narrowed reads** (chosen). Each narrowed request runs its query.
+
+## Decision
+
+A repository read narrowed by a caller-supplied value is not cached. Unnarrowed veterinarian reads stay cached as before.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- No filter value becomes a cache key, so the cache's size stays independent of the filter values callers send.
+- A narrowed request costs one query against a small table. Neither surface is on a hot path.
+- A future filter on another shown attribute inherits this rule unless a later ADR bounds the cache.
+- The page number of unnarrowed reads is also caller-chosen and still keys the cache. That pre-existing exposure is unchanged here.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md § Contracts](../system-design.md#contracts) — the `VetRepository` row
+- [system-design.md § Threat Model](../system-design.md#threat-model) — the cache-growth row
